{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     post/parent — a course, a training module or a docs section.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     ONE PARTIAL FOR ALL THREE, because they are one shape: an overview page
     whose body explains the thing and whose main content is THE LIST OF ITS
     CHILDREN. Only the words differ, and words are a parameter.

     PARAMETERS
       kind       kicker above the title — "Course", "Module", "Section"
       childWord  singular noun for the count — "lesson", "page"

     ── The hero is NSDS's .ns-chero, exactly ──────────────────────────────
         .ns-chero > __inner > __grid > (__body, __media)
     __media belongs INSIDE __grid. Put it beside __inner and it loses the
     grid column and the 16/9 aspect-ratio that come with it, rendering as a
     full-bleed block several screens tall — a nesting bug that looks like a
     styling bug. `--split` only when there is an image, or the text sits at
     55% width beside an empty column.

     ── The slug==tag rule is what makes this work ─────────────────────────
     A parent's slug equals its own tag's slug, and every child carries that
     tag as its PRIMARY tag. So the parent finds its children by filtering on
     its own slug, and the children nest under it in the URL — one fact doing
     both jobs. `+id:-{{id}}` is not optional: the parent carries its own tag
     too, so without it every course lists itself as its first lesson.

     ── ⚠ ONE {{#get}}, AND IT IS DELIBERATE ──────────────────────────────
     The child count would read better in the hero, and it is in the Contents
     head instead. A SECOND {{#get}} in the hero returned nothing however it
     was written — with `as |kids|`, bare with {{pagination.total}}, and
     hoisted to wrap the whole partial. The same filter returns rows through
     the Content API and through the query below, so the filter is not the
     problem; a second async {{#get}} in that position is.

     Two further rules learned the hard way and worth keeping:
       · ALWAYS `as |…|`. Without it the helper takes over the block context
         and the surrounding markup stops seeing the post — output vanishes
         rather than erroring.
       · NEVER as a partial's outermost block. There it resolves to its
         inverse even when the query has rows.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
<article class="{{post_class}}">

    <section class="ns-chero{{#if feature_image}} ns-chero--split{{/if}}">
        <div class="ns-chero__inner">
            <div class="ns-chero__grid">

                <div class="ns-chero__body">
                    <p class="ns-chero__kicker">{{#if kind}}{{kind}}{{else}}{{t "Overview"}}{{/if}}</p>
                    <h1 class="ns-chero__title">{{title}}</h1>
                    {{#if custom_excerpt}}<p class="ns-chero__lede">{{custom_excerpt}}</p>{{/if}}

                    <div class="ns-chero__meta">
                        <span class="ns-tag sp-optional">{{~> "internal/level"~}}</span>
                        <span class="sp-optional">{{~> "internal/duration"~}}</span>
                    </div>

                    {{#if @site.members_enabled}}
                        <div class="ns-chero__actions">{{> "chrome/members"}}</div>
                    {{/if}}
                </div>

                {{#if feature_image}}
                    <div class="ns-chero__media">
                        {{> "media" src=feature_image eager=true
                                    sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 34rem, 100vw"
                                    alt=(concat "" feature_image_alt)}}
                    </div>
                {{/if}}

            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{> "post/body"}}

    {{!-- The children, in publication order. Backdate a post to reorder it —
          that is the whole ordering mechanism, and it is why the fixture gives
          everything spaced-out dates rather than identical ones.

          ⚠ limit="all" IS NOT SUPPORTED by {{#get}}: Ghost returns 15 and says
          nothing. 100 is more children than any sane parent has. --}}
    {{#get "posts" filter="tags:{{slug}}+id:-{{id}}" limit="100" order="published_at asc" include="tags" as |children|}}
        <section class="ns-band ns-band--sunken">
            <div class="ns-band__inner">

                <div class="ns-band__head ns-band__head--between">
                    <h2 class="ns-band__title">{{t "Contents"}}</h2>
                    {{!-- ⚠ NOT {{plural}}. The obvious
                              {{plural children.length empty=(concat …) …}}
                          silently rendered NOTHING — and it took the entire
                          {{#get}} block with it, so the whole curriculum
                          disappeared rather than just the count. The nested
                          (concat (t …) childWord …) subexpressions are what
                          it chokes on; {{children.length}} on its own is 3.

                          A failure that deletes its own container is worth
                          avoiding even at the cost of a clumsier expression,
                          so the words are passed in already pluralised and
                          {{#match}} picks one. It is also more translatable:
                          not every language pluralises by adding an s.

                          ⚠ AND NOTE THE `../`. {{#get}} SWITCHES THE CONTEXT
                          even when `as |children|` is used — the block param
                          is added, but `this` becomes the result set, so the
                          partial's own parameters (childWord, kind) are not
                          visible inside the block without stepping out. That
                          is also why {{plural}} failed here: childWord was
                          undefined, not malformed. Property access across
                          `../` is fine; calling a HELPER across it is not. --}}
                    {{!-- Suppressed at zero: the empty state below already
                          says there is nothing here, and "0 lessons" beside
                          it is the same news twice. --}}
                    {{#match children.length ">" 0}}
                        <p class="ns-band__lede">
                            {{children.length}}
                            {{#match children.length "=" 1}}{{../../childWord}}{{else}}{{../../childWordPlural}}{{/match}}
                        </p>
                    {{/match}}
                </div>

                {{#if children}}
                    <div class="ns-units">
                        {{#foreach children}}{{> "cards/unit" currentSlug=""}}{{/foreach}}
                    </div>
                {{else}}
                    {{!-- A parent with nothing under it yet. A page that is
                          simply blank below the fold reads as broken; saying
                          so reads as new. --}}
                    <p class="ns-empty">{{t "Nothing published here yet."}}</p>
                {{/if}}

            </div>
        </section>
    {{/get}}

</article>
